#plant-header("Daylilies (Mixed)", "Hemerocallis spp.", "daylily")[
  Daylilies are one of the most adaptable, low-maintenance perennials available. Each flower lasts one day, but a single plant produces dozens of blooms over a 3–6 week period depending on variety. Extremely tough: tolerates clay, drought, heat, and neglect once established. Located on the east side of the house with morning sun only — afternoon shade begins around midday. Bloom count will be somewhat lower than catalog photos suggest, which are typically shot in full sun; this is expected and normal, not a sign of plant stress. Fully established since 2022 — on drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with 1 × 2 gph emitter (0.67 gal/cycle) — audit the existing emitter before the 2026 season; if a smaller emitter is installed, swap to 2 gph before reducing overall run time. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule. As fully established clumps (since 2022) in afternoon shade, water demand is lower than full-sun daylilies. Daylilies are very drought tolerant after establishment; consistent moisture during the blooming period (June–July) improves flower production, but the shared schedule is sufficient. Stop drip support in September as temperatures drop.

=== Fertilization

Per soil profile, use N-K formula only — no bloom boosters. As fully established clumps, light fertilization is appropriate, not heavy. In early spring, apply a light N-K formula (16-0-8 or ammonium sulfate paired with potassium sulfate) to address your low nitrogen and low potassium. Potassium supports flower production. Apply a chelated micronutrient package in spring — very low iron and zinc will reduce vigor and bloom quality if not addressed. Avoid excess nitrogen, which promotes foliage over flowers.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Deadhead spent flower scapes (the whole stem) at the base once all buds on that scape have opened, to maintain a tidy appearance and redirect energy. After all blooming is complete, cut all foliage back by one-third for a cleaner look. In fall, cut to the ground or leave for winter protection — both approaches work. Divide clumps every 4–5 years in spring or early fall when they become crowded and flowering decreases.

=== Wildlife Value

Daylily flowers are visited by native bees, bumble bees, and occasional butterflies during their brief daily opening. Hummingbirds occasionally probe the orange and red varieties. The dense foliage clumps provide ground-level cover for cottontail rabbits, who sometimes shelter there but do not eat established daylily foliage (it is mildly toxic to them). Birds rarely feed on daylilies directly. Field mice may occasionally burrow near the dense root clumps.

=== Pests & Disease

Daylilies have excellent pest resistance in Colorado's dry climate. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=daylily+thrips+treatment")[Thrips] can cause streaked, distorted flower petals — treat with neem oil or spinosad. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=spider+mites+daylily+treatment")[Spider mites] occasionally occur in hot, dry summers — look for stippling on foliage; treat with horticultural oil. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=daylily+rust+treatment")[Daylily rust] (orange pustules on foliage) is rare in Colorado's dry climate but can occur — remove affected foliage and treat with a copper or sulfur fungicide. The yellow variety 'Stella de Oro' is particularly robust.

=== Other Care

Daylilies spread slowly by rhizome. Plan for clumps to reach 2–3 feet in diameter over time. They naturalize well along slopes and borders. Mix early, mid, and late-season varieties for an extended bloom period from June through August.
